---
/**
 * foodcraft / TestimonialsBM — "Tahanan Cafe" FROM THE REGULARS: a CENTRED head
 * (the signature underlined walnut eyebrow over a Libre Caslon Text headline)
 * above a three-up row of quote cards drawn on the WHITE ground with a hairline
 * box and a 2px corner — each card opened by a rating mark, then the quote in
 * Libre Caslon Text at 19px, and a tracked-caps attribution pinned to the
 * bottom of the card.
 *
 * ROLE: the social proof a stranger reads before deciding to walk in — which is
 * exactly why this file prints nothing the owner did not put on record.
 *
 * THE DESIGN IS THE SPECIFICATION. Every value below is lifted verbatim from the
 * `data-component="TESTIMONIALS"` element of "Tahanan Cafe Landing.dc.html":
 *   head          text-align:center, margin-bottom 40px
 *   eyebrow       10px / .34em / caps / 700 / #7A4E2D, sitting ON a 1px #E0D3C6
 *                 rule with 9px of padding under it   → THE .kick PRIMITIVE,
 *                 restated NOWHERE here. The underline is the single most
 *                 recognisable mark in this design and it lives on the shared
 *                 primitive; a section that re-declares the eyebrow loses it.
 *   headline      Libre Caslon Text 400, clamp(29px,3.8vw,50px)/1.14,
 *                 margin .26em 0 0 — identical to the wrapper's .h2 except that
 *                 the design's margin is on TOP and the block is centred, so
 *                 only the margin/alignment is overridden below.
 *   grid          repeat(3, minmax(0,1fr)), gap 24px
 *   card          <figure>, margin 0, 1px #EDE6DF hairline, radius 2px,
 *                 padding 30px 28px, display:flex, column, gap 14px.
 *                 NO fill: the design gives the figure no background, so the
 *                 white page shows through. That is deliberate and is not a
 *                 missing --card declaration — this design draws its grids with
 *                 BORDERS, not with filled tiles.
 *   mark          13px, #8A5A34, letter-spacing 3px
 *   quote         Libre Caslon Text 19px / 1.6, #241B14, margin 0
 *   attribution   margin-top auto, 11px / .2em / caps / 600 / #6B6057
 * Mapped to the wrapper tokens carrying those exact values: #FFFFFF = --paper,
 * #EDE6DF = --line, #E0D3C6 = --line-2, 2px = --r, #8A5A34 = --accent-2,
 * #241B14 = --ink, #6B6057 = --muted, #7A4E2D = --accent, Libre Caslon Text =
 * --disp, Karla = --sans. NO LITERAL HEX APPEARS BELOW, and nothing is
 * re-derived to "survive" a colour scheme — this is the card the designer drew.
 *
 * WHY NOT .mono FOR THE ATTRIBUTION: the shared .mono is 10px / .2em / 700 and
 * takes --muted. The design's figcaption here is 11px / .2em / 600. Half a
 * weight and a pixel apart, so it is written scoped rather than borrowed —
 * borrowing would quietly ship a different line than the design specifies.
 *
 * NOT A BAND. The closing CTA band is the only dark ground in this design; the
 * testimonials sit on plain white. So this file carries no `band-dark`, and the
 * --band / --band-kick / --band-body / --on-band trio is untouched here.
 *
 * CLASS PREFIX `tah-`, chosen for THIS template. It is deliberately NOT the
 * `rg-` set from TestimonialsBL: an audit had to rename a whole class set that
 * had been copied verbatim from the template before it, and two templates
 * sharing a scoped class name makes every future diff read as a shared
 * component that is not one. Only the house STYLE was copied from BL — the doc
 * block, the coercion helpers, the guard discipline.
 *
 * CLASS-NAME NOTE (theme engine): the section must NOT be class "testi" —
 * buildOverrideCss pins `.testi, .testi p { color: … !important }` and would
 * repaint the quotes with a scheme colour this design never chose. It is
 * "tah-reviews", and it emits no button at all, so no engine label pin
 * (.btn-primary/.btn-ghost/.nav-cta/.cta-band/.trust) reaches anything here.
 *
 * READS content.testimonials.items ({quote, who, rating}); tolerates the legacy
 * bare array parked at content.testimonials and the legacy author/name
 * spellings for the attribution. The data-field always names the CANONICAL
 * path, so the first sidebar edit migrates a legacy row onto the real key.
 *
 * COERCED AT EVERY READ — THE ROW FIRST, THEN EVERY FIELD. Nothing upstream
 * type-checks either one. astro-builder's normalizeBlock passes a non-object
 * entry straight through (`if (!it || typeof it !== 'object') return it`), so
 * `items` can hold a null, and reading `.quote` off that null throws at BUILD
 * time and takes the whole page down. So each row is normalised to an object
 * before a single field is read, and a bare STRING row is read as the quote
 * itself — the legacy shape where the row IS the quote — so owner text is never
 * silently dropped. Then the fields: a quote arriving as a number crashes the
 * build on .trim(); one arriving as an object prints "[object Object]" through
 * set:html. str() passes strings and finite numbers and treats everything else
 * as absent. Blank-after-trim counts as absent too — a whitespace-only `who`
 * must not draw an empty figcaption, and a whitespace-only tag must not draw an
 * empty underlined eyebrow (an eyebrow with no word is a bare floating rule).
 * normalizeBlock aliases name/author onto `who` only when `who` is null, so a
 * row carrying who:'' keeps its blank; the fallback chain below is `||` and not
 * `??` so that row still prints the name the owner did type.
 *
 * EDITABLE HOOKS (every one declared in components/editor/genericContentSchema.ts,
 * group "testimonials" — a data-field on a path the schema does not own is a
 * dead hook that no sidebar input can focus)
 *   testimonials.tag                  — the underlined walnut eyebrow
 *   testimonials.headline             — set:html, so an owner can place this
 *                                       design's second signature, the REAL
 *                                       Libre Caslon Text ITALIC WALNUT
 *                                       emphasis, anywhere inside their own
 *                                       sentence (the wrapper styles .h2 em)
 *   testimonials.items.${i}.quote     — set:html, the serif blockquote
 *   testimonials.items.${i}.who       — the tracked-caps figcaption
 *   testimonials.items.${i}.rating    — read, never printed as text: it is the
 *                                       ONLY thing that can draw a star (below).
 *                                       It deliberately carries NO data-field on
 *                                       the star row, and that is not a missing
 *                                       hook — the row RENDERS the number, it is
 *                                       not the number. editorBridge's ed:update
 *                                       would replace the glyphs with the raw
 *                                       digit, and VisualEditor (which does not
 *                                       honour data-derived) makes every
 *                                       [data-field] contentEditable, so an
 *                                       admin could type stars back into rating
 *                                       and lose the score. It is edited in the
 *                                       sidebar's "Rating out of 5" box, which
 *                                       genericContentSchema declares.
 *
 * DERIVED, not authored: the number of stars, and the reveal delay (capped at 3
 * because the wrapper only defines delays 1-3, so a fourth card must not wait).
 *
 * ANTI-FABRICATION — the hard rule of this section:
 *   - THE STAR ROW IS NOT HARDCODED. The design prints five stars over every
 *     quote, because the designer was filling in their own template. Five stars
 *     for a customer who never gave a score is a claim invented on the owner's
 *     behalf, so stars are drawn ONLY from a finite numeric
 *     testimonials.items.${i}.rating. Non-numeric, blank and absent ratings draw
 *     no stars at all; the card instead opens with a short walnut hairline that
 *     occupies the same beat and states nothing — which also suits a design
 *     whose whole grammar is hairlines. Expect that hairline on essentially
 *     every real site: it is the correct, honest default, not a missing feature.
 *   - CLAMPING, stated so it is not read as an oversight: a rating is rounded,
 *     then capped at 5 (a typo of "7" draws five stars, never seven). A value
 *     that rounds BELOW 1 — 0, -2, 0.4 — is treated as no usable rating and
 *     draws the hairline. It is never clamped UP to one star, because inventing
 *     a star out of a zero is the same fabrication as inventing five.
 *   - The accessible name announces the SAME number that is drawn, so the label
 *     and the glyphs can never disagree.
 *   - NO review count, NO average score, NO platform name, NO "regulars' choice"
 *     badge. None of it is in the data contract, and the design draws none.
 *   - Quote and attribution are guarded independently and every guard sits
 *     OUTSIDE its tag, so an unattributed quote renders as a quote rather than
 *     as an empty caption holding the card's bottom gap open.
 *   - A row with no quote is DROPPED: the card IS the quote, so a name-only row
 *     would draw a hairline box attributing nothing to somebody. The row stays
 *     in the sidebar and reappears the instant a quote is typed.
 *
 * SECTION GATE: the whole <section> renders only when at least one row has a
 * real quote. It deliberately does NOT count tag/headline. Those two arrive
 * from the admin sidebar or the AI extractor with nothing behind them all the
 * time, and lib/derive-content-defaults.ts fills heads across the site, so a
 * gate counting a headline would ship a centred heading over an empty grid —
 * the one thing a proof section must never do.
 *
 * ORIGINAL INDEXES: `i` is captured BEFORE blank rows are dropped, so deleting
 * row 2 cannot misdirect an edit of row 3 into row 4. Nothing here is numbered
 * on screen, so no display index is needed.
 *
 * THE ONE AUTHORED STRING in this file is the stars' aria-label, which is never
 * visible. It exists because a row of glyphs with no accessible name is
 * decoration announcing nothing, and a rating the owner typed is information a
 * screen-reader user is entitled to. No visible word on this page comes from
 * this file.
 *
 * DELIBERATELY NOT RENDERED:
 *   testimonials.source   — the schema declares it, but this template already
 *                           has an honest home for a star claim (rating), and
 *                           the design draws no source line under the head. A
 *                           "Reviews on Google" line would also be a platform
 *                           claim nothing in the pipeline verifies.
 *   testimonials.bigQuote — there is no pull-quote element in this design.
 *   testimonials.items.${i}.role — the row schema stops at quote/who/rating, so
 *                           the hook would focus nothing in the v3 sidebar.
 *   No image: the design's card is type only, with no avatar plate — so there
 *   is no .plate, no .ph, no .cap, no role="img" on a photo and nothing that
 *   would need an owner-authored accessible name.
 *
 * NO ANCHOR HELPER, and that is not an omission. The design's TESTIMONIALS
 * element contains no <a> at all, so this file emits no href and owns no
 * data-href-field. Copying onPage()/PAGE_ANCHORS/ANCHOR_ALIAS in here would be
 * an unused function rather than a safeguard — the same call TestimonialsBL
 * made, for the same reason. The section also takes NO id: the page's ids are
 * menu / about / gallery / visit / faq / main, and the design gives this
 * section none of them.
 *
 * CONTRAST (sRGB relative luminance, recomputed against this design's native
 * palette on the white ground — NOT copied from the wrapper's table, which
 * understates several pairs and labels one of them wrongly):
 *   quote --ink on --paper 16.9 · stars --accent-2 on --paper 5.85 ·
 *   eyebrow --accent on --paper 7.11 · attribution --muted on --paper 6.11.
 *   Nothing here is below AA. The wrapper's frontmatter prints 14.9 for
 *   ink/paper and 5.71 for "muted/paper" — 5.71 is in fact --muted on --tint
 *   (5.72), a surface this section never uses. Both of its figures are LOWER
 *   than the truth, so its "nothing is below the bar" conclusion still holds;
 *   only the numbers are wrong.
 *   --muted is remapped by the engine to palette.inkSoft, so the attribution
 *   figure is the native value and not a guarantee under every scheme.
 *
 * --accent-2 IS INVISIBLE TO THE ENGINE, which is worth stating because the
 * wrapper's frontmatter says buildOverrideCss wins "accent*" and that reads as
 * covering this token. It does not: the engine emits --accent, --accent-rgb,
 * --accent-light, --accent-soft and --accent-deep, and nothing named
 * --accent-2. So under a scheme pick the eyebrow moves and the star mark keeps
 * the design's #8A5A34 walnut. That is the right outcome for a mark whose whole
 * job is to sit quietly beside the quote, but it is a divergence a reader would
 * otherwise assume was an oversight.
 *
 * VERTICAL RHYTHM comes from the wrapper's shared `section` padding; the
 * design's "84px top, 0 bottom" is that same rhythm once every section stacks.
 */
interface Props { content: any; layout?: any; }
const { content } = Astro.props;

const test = content.testimonials || {};

/* Coerce at EVERY read — see COERCED AT EVERY READ above. A string passes; a
   finite number passes as its digits; null, undefined, NaN, an array and an
   object are not content and become ''. Nothing downstream calls a string
   method on raw input, so no shape from the pipeline can fail the build. */
const str = (v: unknown): string =>
  typeof v === 'string' ? v : (typeof v === 'number' && Number.isFinite(v) ? String(v) : '');

const tag = str(test.tag).trim();
const headline = str(test.headline).trim();

type Item = {
  quote?: unknown;
  who?: unknown;
  author?: unknown;
  name?: unknown;
  rating?: unknown;
};

/* Tolerate both shapes at every read: the canonical object with .items, and the
   legacy bare array parked at content.testimonials. */
const items: Item[] = Array.isArray(test.items)
  ? test.items
  : (Array.isArray(test) ? (test as Item[]) : []);

/* A rating is a CLAIM about a customer. Only a finite number becomes stars; it
   is rounded, capped at 5, and anything that rounds below 1 yields 0 — which
   draws the hairline, not a star. Never widen this.
   READ THROUGH str(), the same gate every other field here uses: a bare
   Number() is wider than this file's own rule, because Number(true) is 1 and
   Number(['5']) is 5 — so a boolean or a single-element array arriving from the
   extractor would have printed a star nobody typed. str() passes strings and
   finite numbers and nothing else. */
const starsOf = (it: Item): number => {
  const r = Number(str(it.rating).trim());
  if (!Number.isFinite(r)) return 0;
  const n = Math.round(r);
  return n >= 1 ? Math.min(5, n) : 0;
};

const STAR = '★';

/* Normalise ONCE, carrying the ORIGINAL array index, then drop the rows that
   cannot render an honest card. Gating on `rows` rather than `items` also means
   a block of blank rows hides instead of shipping three empty hairline boxes. */
const rows = items
  .map((raw, i: number) => {
    /* THE ROW IS COERCED BEFORE ANY FIELD IS READ. A null row would throw on
       `.quote` and fail the build; a bare string row is the quote the owner
       typed and is kept, not dropped. */
    const it: Item = raw && typeof raw === 'object' ? (raw as Item) : ({ quote: raw } as Item);
    return {
      /* the quote keeps its own leading/trailing space — only the GUARD is
         trimmed, so a blank-looking quote cannot open a hairline card */
      quote: str(it.quote),
      who: str(it.who).trim() || str(it.author).trim() || str(it.name).trim(),
      stars: starsOf(it),
      i,
    };
  })
  .filter((r) => Boolean(r.quote.trim()));

const hasHead = Boolean(tag || headline);
---
{rows.length > 0 && (
  <section class="tah-reviews">
    <div class="wrap">
      {hasHead && (
        <div class="tah-head reveal">
          {tag && <span class="kick" data-field="testimonials.tag">{tag}</span>}
          {headline && <h2 class="h2" data-field="testimonials.headline" set:html={headline}></h2>}
        </div>
      )}
      <div class="tah-grid">
        {rows.map((row, k) => (
          <figure class="tah-card reveal" data-delay={String(Math.min(k, 3))}>
            {row.stars > 0 ? (
              /* Drawn from the owner's own number, and named with that same
                 number so the glyphs are never decoration-only. */
              <span class="tah-stars" role="img" aria-label={`Rated ${row.stars} out of 5`}>
                <span aria-hidden="true">{STAR.repeat(row.stars)}</span>
              </span>
            ) : (
              /* Where the design hardcodes five stars. A walnut hairline on the
                 same beat: same visual opening, same card height, no claim. */
              <span class="tah-rule" aria-hidden="true"></span>
            )}
            <blockquote data-field={`testimonials.items.${row.i}.quote`} set:html={row.quote}></blockquote>
            {row.who && (
              <figcaption data-field={`testimonials.items.${row.i}.who`}>{row.who}</figcaption>
            )}
          </figure>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
)}
<style>
  /* ── head · centred, 40px of air under it (design) ───────────────────────── */
  .tah-head { text-align: center; margin-bottom: 40px; }
  /* The eyebrow is NOT restated here — only wrapped. Restating .kick's font or
     colour would drop the border-bottom that IS the signature. */
  .tah-head .kick { overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
  /* The wrapper's .h2 is left-aligned with a BOTTOM margin; the design's head is
     centred with the margin on TOP. The 24ch cap from the wrapper is kept and
     centred by the auto margins so a long owner headline cannot run the full
     width of the container. No font-size is touched at ANY breakpoint here, so
     this section cannot re-introduce the clamp inversion an audit found on the
     previous template (a 900-query floor sitting above a 520-query cap). */
  .tah-reviews .h2 {
    margin: 0.26em auto 0;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
  /* set:html output carries no scope attribute, so an owner's <em> inside the
     headline is only reachable through :global(). The wrapper already paints
     .h2 em italic walnut; this rule exists so the .em spelling behaves the same
     when the emphasis arrives from the editor. */
  .tah-reviews .h2 :global(em),
  .tah-reviews .h2 :global(.em) { font-style: italic; color: var(--accent); }

  /* ── grid · three up, 24px (design) ──────────────────────────────────────── */
  .tah-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: 24px;
  }

  /* ── card · the design's hairline box on the white ground, SHARP 2px corner.
        No background: the design gives the figure none, and this template draws
        its grids with borders rather than fills. The flex column with a 14px
        gap is the design's own spacing mechanism — it is what lets the
        attribution be pushed to the bottom edge with margin-top:auto so a short
        quote and a long one still line their names up across a row. ───────── */
  .tah-card {
    margin: 0;
    min-width: 0;
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: var(--r);
    padding: 30px 28px;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 14px;
  }

  /* ── the rating mark. Both variants occupy the same ~23px beat so a card WITH
        a rating and a card without stay on the same baseline in the same row.
        line-height is DECLARED rather than inherited so that height is
        deterministic and does not move with the body leading — and 1.78 is
        precisely the value it WOULD inherit from the wrapper's body, so this
        declaration changes nothing on screen. The design leaves its own mark row
        at `normal` (its body sets no line-height at all), which is a hair
        tighter; that gap belongs to the wrapper's shared body leading, applies
        to every BM section, and is not re-derived here. ──────────────────── */
  .tah-stars {
    display: block;
    font-size: 13px;
    line-height: 1.78;
    letter-spacing: 3px;
    color: var(--accent-2);
  }
  /* The hairline that replaces the design's hardcoded star row. 74px is roughly
     the width five glyphs occupy at 13px on 3px tracking, and the 11px margins
     reproduce the 23px the star row takes. */
  .tah-rule {
    display: block;
    width: 74px;
    max-width: 100%;
    height: 1px;
    background-color: var(--accent-2);
    margin: 11px 0;
  }

  /* ── quote · Libre Caslon Text at the design's 19px. The UA gives <blockquote>
        a 40px side margin, so the reset here is load-bearing, not tidying. The
        wrapper's bare-element `p` reset is (0,1,1), which is why every rule in
        this block is qualified by a section or card class. ────────────────── */
  .tah-card blockquote {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--disp);
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: 19px;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--ink);
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
  /* An owner's <em> inside a quote stays the quote's own ink. The italic walnut
     emphasis is the HEADLINE signature; repainting a customer's words walnut
     would read as the cafe talking, not the customer. */
  .tah-card blockquote :global(em),
  .tah-card blockquote :global(.em) { font-style: italic; color: inherit; }

  /* ── attribution · the design's 11px / .2em / 600, pinned to the card floor.
        Written scoped rather than borrowed from .mono (10px / .2em / 700). ── */
  .tah-card figcaption {
    margin-top: auto;
    font-family: var(--sans);
    font-weight: 600;
    font-size: 11px;
    letter-spacing: 0.2em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--muted);
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  /* ── RESPONSIVE · this section owns its own grid; the wrapper owns the shared
        760 / 520 type pass. Nothing here may push the page sideways: every text
        node above already carries overflow-wrap:anywhere, and there is no
        button in this section to hold a nowrap label. ─────────────────────── */
  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .tah-grid { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: 20px; }
  }
  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .tah-grid { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); }
  }
  @media (max-width: 520px) {
    .tah-head { margin-bottom: 32px; }
    .tah-reviews .h2 { max-width: none; }
    .tah-card { padding: 24px 20px; }
    /* 19px to 18px, and the same beat kept: font-size only ever DECREASES as the
       viewport narrows, at every breakpoint in this file. */
    .tah-card blockquote { font-size: 18px; line-height: 1.55; }
    .tah-card figcaption { letter-spacing: 0.16em; }
    .tah-stars { letter-spacing: 2px; }
  }
</style>
